</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this Section, we conduct various experiments to have a further comprehensive understanding about the backdoor-powered prompt injection attack.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS5.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.5.1 </span>Original Input Instruction Ignoring</h4>

<div id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, we aim to explore whether existing prompt injection attack methods, as well as the backdoor-powered prompt injection attack, can successfully induce an LLM to ignore the original input instruction and exclusively execute the injected instruction. We conduct experiments with the general injection task without applying any defenses. Our primary focus is on whether responses include answers to the original input instructions. The results are presented in Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.5.1 Original Input Instruction Ignoring ‣ 5.5 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Backdoor-Powered Prompt Injection Attacks Nullify Defense Methods" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.
From the table, we observe that while the primary design goals of the “Ignore attack,” “Escape attack,” “Fake completion attack,” and “Combined attack” are to deceive the LLM into disregarding the original input instruction and executing the injected instruction, their effectiveness in achieving this is less than satisfactory. In contrast, the backdoor-powered prompt injection attack demonstrates a much higher ignoring effectiveness, almost completely deceiving the LLM into ignoring the original input instruction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
